Detailed Findings
Site Architecture (Single-Page Static)
AirtelBlack is a one-page static website. On load it shows a branded “⚠️ Website Offline” splash screen (3-second Airtel-style animation), then reveals the main page with anchor links. The top nav (e.g. Services, CEO Letter, RFCs…) are in-page anchors (href="#services", etc.). The content is organized in <section> blocks (with headings <h2>, <h3>). A “Skip to main content” link at page start indicates an accessible structure. The header branding (“Your issue has been resolved ignored.”) uses CSS to strike out “resolved”. The layout adapts responsively (media queries or flex/grid likely).

Splash / Counter: After load, a red circular spinner appears with text “Preparing your Airtel Black experience…”, then transitions to show dynamic counters. These counters (e.g. “SRs Generated”, “Days of Patience”) update in real-time via JS timers. This real-time counter feature and initial splash are custom scripts (no evidence of library).
Interactive Sections: The page includes interactive bits: e.g. a looping text “ticket responses” ticker, and a Bingo board (“click squares”) where clicking cells marks them (persisted in localStorage). These behaviors strongly indicate plain JS + CSS; no SPA frameworks.
Navigation: Links in the nav and footer jump to sections by ID (one-page nav). There are no distinct HTML pages to load – the page does not navigate away. We confirm it’s a Single-Page layout by inspecting anchor links and noticing the entire content is in the one HTML.
